# Cursor Rules for Frontend Repository

## General Principles
- Write clear, maintainable code following React and TypeScript best practices
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Keep components focused and single-purpose
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Document complex business logic and non-obvious decisions
- Follow the existing code patterns and conventions

## TypeScript

### Type Definitions
- Use TypeScript strict mode; avoid `any` types
- Use `type` over `interface`
- Use explicit return types for functions when not obvious
- Define props types inline or as separate types
- Use generic types for reusable components/hooks
- Leverage utility types (`Pick`, `Omit`, `Partial`, etc.) when appropriate

### Type Safety
- Always type function parameters and return values
- Use type guards for runtime type checking
- Prefer `unknown` over `any` when type is truly unknown
- Use `as const` for literal types when needed
- Avoid type assertions (`as`) unless necessary; prefer type guards

## React Components

### Component Structure
- Use functional components with hooks
- Keep components small and focused (ideally < 200 lines)
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Use `memo` for expensive components that re-render frequently
- Prefer named exports for components: `export const ComponentName = () => { ... }`

### Conditional Rendering
- Prefer ternary operators over `&&` for conditional rendering in JSX
- Use `{condition ? <Component /> : null}` instead of `{condition && <Component />}`
- This avoids potential issues with falsy values (0, '') being rendered

### Props and State
- Define props types at the top of the file
- Use `React.PropsWithChildren` when components accept children
- Prefer controlled components over uncontrolled
- Use `useState` for local component state
- Lift state up when multiple components need it
- Use context for deeply nested prop drilling

### Component Organization
- Order: imports, types, component, exports
- Group related imports together (React, third-party, local)
- Use path aliases (`~` for `src/`) for local imports
- Keep component files co-located with related files (hooks, types, utils)

## Hooks

### Custom Hooks
- Prefix custom hooks with `use` (e.g., `useCustomThings`)
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Return objects for multiple values, tuples for 2-3 related values
- Keep hooks focused on a single concern
- Document hook parameters and return values

### Hook Dependencies
- Always include all dependencies in dependency arrays
- Use `useCallback` for functions passed as props or in dependency arrays
- Use `useMemo` for expensive computations
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies; prefer stable references

### Common Patterns
- Use `useQuery` from React Query for data fetching
- Use `useMutation` for data mutations
- Use `useToggle` from UI library for boolean state
- Use `useEffectOnce` for effects that should run once

## Data Fetching

### React Query (TanStack Query)
- Use `useQuery` for GET requests and data fetching
- Use `useMutation` for POST/PUT/DELETE operations
- Define query keys as arrays: `['resource', id, ...filters]`
- Use `enabled` option to conditionally fetch data
- Handle loading and error states appropriately
- Use query invalidation after mutations

### GraphQL (Apollo Client)
- Use Apollo hooks (`useQuery`, `useMutation`) for GraphQL operations
- Define queries/mutations in separate files or use `gql` template literals
- Handle loading and error states from Apollo hooks
- Use fragments for reusable query parts

## Forms

### React Final Form
- Use `react-final-form` for form management
- Define form validation functions separately
- Use field-level validation when possible
- Handle form submission with proper error handling
- Use `FormSpy` for accessing form state when needed

## Styling

### Tailwind CSS
- Use Tailwind utility classes for styling
- Use `tailwind-merge` for conditional class merging
- Prefer utility classes over custom CSS
- Use CSS variables for theme values when needed
- Keep component-specific styles minimal

## Testing
- See `testing.mdc` for unit test (Vitest) and E2E (Playwright/Cypress) conventions.

## Internationalization (i18n)

### Lingui
- Use `Trans` component for translatable text
- Use `t` macro for string literals in code
- Extract all user-facing strings for translation
- Use descriptive message IDs
- Keep translations in sync with code changes

## Code Organization

### File Structure
- Co-locate related files (component, hooks, types, utils)
- Use index files for clean imports
- Group by feature/domain, not by file type
- Keep shared utilities in `src/utils/`
- Keep shared hooks in `src/hooks/`

### Imports
- Order imports: React, third-party, local
- Use absolute imports with path aliases (`~`)
- Group related imports together
- Remove unused imports

## Performance

### Optimization
- Use `React.memo` for expensive components
- Use `useMemo` for expensive computations
- Use `useCallback` for stable function references
- Lazy load routes and heavy components
- Optimize images and assets
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders

### Bundle Size
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting
- Lazy load routes with `React.lazy`
- Avoid importing entire libraries when only a function is needed
- Monitor bundle size regularly

## Error Handling

### Error Boundaries
- Use error boundaries for component error handling
- Provide fallback UI for errors
- Log errors appropriately (e.g., Sentry)

### API Errors
- Handle API errors gracefully
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Retry failed requests when appropriate
- Use React Query error handling patterns

## Accessibility

### A11y Best Practices
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Provide proper ARIA labels when needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Maintain proper focus management
- Test with screen readers when possible
- Use proper heading hierarchy

## Code Quality

### Linting and Formatting
- Follow ESLint rules (no warnings allowed)
- Use Prettier for code formatting
- Fix linting errors before committing
- Use TypeScript strict mode

### Git Practices
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Include {{TRACKER}} ticket ids in commits
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Review code before merging
